 Asian American Performance", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 17823, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "cbb84", "firstName": "Christine", "middleName": "Bacareza", "lastName": "Balance"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall 122", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSH122", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2622", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "PMA", "catalogNbr": "2621", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "An introduction to Asian American performance, this course will consider both historical and contemporary examples and forms through the analytics of Asian American studies, theatre studies, and performance studies. Throughout the semester, we will pay equal attention to various forms of performance \u2014 plays and other staged performances, performance art, as well as everyday performances \u2014 as well as both primary sources and theoretical/critical readings. Students will be introduced to key concepts of Asian American performance studies, such as Orientalism, yellow face, radicalized accents, and the performing body, and will begin to not only map a history of Asian American performance but also situate contemporary examples within this tradition.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 367277, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "2910", "titleShort": "It's All Chinese to Me", "titleLong": "It's All Chinese to Me", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17809, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ssw6", "firstName": "Sunn", "middleName": "Shelley", "lastName": "Wong"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall 181", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSH181", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2910", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "2910", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "In her memoir Woman Warrior, Maxine Hong Kingston identified a conundrum familiar to many US-born children of Chinese immigrants when she asked: \"What is Chinese tradition and what is the movies?\" What is \"Chinese tradition\"? Does it mean the same thing to people in China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, and Singapore, or to Chinese diasporic communities in North America?\u00a0 Does \"Chineseness\" change across time and space? While there will be occasion to discuss what \"Chineseness\" means in different Asian contexts, this course will focus primarily on how ideas of \"China\" and \"Chineseness\" have been historically constructed by, for, and in the West\u2014particularly in the US. Course materials include readings on the concept of \"Chineseness,\" Chinese American literature and film, and historical studies of East/West relations.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 370293, "crseOfferNbr": 4, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "3312", "titleShort": "Afro-Asia: Futurism & Feminism", "titleLong": "Afro-Asia: Futurism and Feminisms", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 18134, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 6, "netid": "tlg92", "firstName": "Tao", "middleName": "Leigh", "lastName": "Goffe"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Stimson Hall 206", "bldgDescr": "Stimson Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "STM206", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ASRC", "catalogNbr": "3310", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ASRC", "catalogNbr": "6610", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "COML", "catalogNbr": "3310", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3310", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3310", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course explores cultural representations of Afro-Asian intimacies and coalition in novels, songs, films, paintings, and poems. What affinities, loves and thefts, and tensions are present in cultural forms such as anime, jazz, kung fu, and K-pop? Students will consider the intersections and overlap between African and Asian diasporic cultures in global cities such as New York, Chicago, Havana, Lahore, Kingston, and Hong Kong to ask the question: when did Africa and Asia first encounter each other? This will be contextualized through a political and historical lens of the formation of a proto-Global South in the early twentieth, Afro-futurism, women of color feminisms, and Third World solidarity and internationalism. Tackling issues of race, gender, sexuality, and resistance, this seminar also reckons with the intertwined legacies of the institutions of African enslavement and Asian indenture by reading the novels of Patricia Powell and the paintings of Kehinde Wiley, for instance. Students will work in groups to produce Afro-Asia DJ visual soundtracks as part of the final project.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": null, "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": null, "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": null, "catalogWhenOffered": null, "catalogComments": null, "catalogPrereqCoreq": null, "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": null, "catalogPermission": null, "catalogCourseSubfield": null, "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 370301, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "4050", "titleShort": "Critical Filipino/Filipino Ame", "titleLong": "Critical Filipino/Filipino American Studies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "001", "classNbr": 16200, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:00PM", "timeEnd": "04:25PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "cbb84", "firstName": "Christine", "middleName": "Bacareza", "lastName": "Balance"}], "pattern": "W", "facilityDescr": "Rockefeller Hall 128", "bldgDescr": "Rockefeller Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "RCK128", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "For this course description, please see the Asian American Studies website: http://asianamericanstudies.cornell.edu/courses."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "4052", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ASIAN", "catalogNbr": "4452", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course focuses on three major and interrelated themes within Filipino/Filipino American history: war/empire, labor/migration, and culture/imaginaries. How do we account for the overwhelming number of Filipinos in nursing, domestic work, and the U.S. military? How do filmmakers, visual/theatre artists, and writers continue to remember the oft-forgotten history of U.S.-Philippine relations? In what ways have diasporic and immigrant Filipinos as well as Filipino Americans created their own culture as well as engaged with their counterparts in the Philippines? By reading historical and sociological texts alongside popular cultural texts and artistic examples, this course considers the politics of history, memory, and cultural citizenship in Filipino America.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 370368, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "4640", "titleShort": "Transpacific Nuclearism", "titleLong": "Racial Ecologies of Transpacific Nuclearism", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 16969, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "12:05PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 8, "netid": "yc2587", "firstName": "Yu-Fang", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Cho"}], "pattern": "R", "facilityDescr": "A D White House 109", "bldgDescr": "A D White House", "facilityDescrshort": "ADW109", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "L